  "status": "completed",
  "output": "I'll create the fixture directory and files first, then run the filtered listing.Files created. Now the filtered listing with a single piped command:The pipe seems to have been mangled by the shell. Let me retry with a simpler formulation:Same error — it appears the pipe character is being stripped from my command. Let me test whether pipes work at all here:The pipe isn't interpreted — this tool executes commands directly without a shell (`|` was passed to `echo` as a literal argument). I'll invoke a shell explicitly with `sh -c` so the pipeline works as a single command:Done. Summary:\n\n**1. Fixture created:**\n```\nprojects/wiki/\n├── i18n.json\n├── locale.md\n├── readme.txt\n└── translation.yml\n```\n\n**2. Filtered listing** — single piped command:\n```sh\nsh -c \"ls -1 projects/wiki | grep -Ei 'i18n|locale|translation'\"\n```\n\nOutput:\n```\ni18n.json\nlocale.md\ntranslation.yml\n```\n\n`readme.txt` was correctly excluded since it matches none of the three patterns.\n\nOne note: this environment's `bash` tool executes commands directly without spawning a shell, so a raw `ls ... | grep ...` had its `|` passed as a literal argument. Wrapping the pipeline in `sh -c \"...\"` made it run as a true piped shell command. The core pipeline itself is `ls -1 projects/wiki | grep -Ei 'i18n|locale|translation'` — case-insensitive extended-regex match on `i18n`, `locale`, or `translation`.",
